exports[`foedrum doctor > reports every tool, a sandbox and a container runtime on Linux, and exits 0 1`] = `
Foedrum doctor

  node         24.20.0
  npm          11.6.2   /usr/bin/npm
  git          2.47.1   /usr/bin/git
  gh           2.102.0  /usr/bin/gh
  claude       2.1.301  /home/brewer/.local/bin/claude
  bwrap        0.9.0    /usr/bin/bwrap
  socat        1.7.4.4  /usr/bin/socat
  slirp4netns  1.2.1    /usr/bin/slirp4netns

Isolation: sandboxed (bubblewrap and socat)
  The sandbox hides your home folder and the other user folders from a brewer's shell commands, apart from the worktree, git's global configuration, parts of ~/.claude and the toolchain folders of serve's PATH, hides gh's hosts file where an absolute GH_CONFIG_DIR or XDG_CONFIG_HOME puts it, and the token variables, and keeps them from reaching GitHub; Claude Code's docs don't say whether the sandbox stops a read from the OS keyring. A login a brewer did read is stopped at the push and left out of the pull request and the status comment when it looks like one. Check steps run in an OS sandbox of their own, with no network and the home folder hidden, so they can't read gh's login.
Container: podman 5.2.0 at /usr/bin/podman; the container level is available

Foedrum has everything it needs.

`;

exports[`foedrum doctor > reports permissions only on native Windows, recommends WSL2, and exits 0 1`] = `
Foedrum doctor

  node    24.20.0
  npm     11.6.2   C:\\Program Files\\nodejs\\npm.cmd
  git     2.47.1   C:\\Program Files\\Git\\cmd\\git.exe
  gh      2.102.0  C:\\Program Files\\GitHub CLI\\gh.exe
  claude  2.1.301  C:\\Users\\brewer\\.local\\bin\\claude.exe

Isolation: permissions only (native Windows has no sandbox for shell commands)
  For untrusted repositories, run Foedrum inside WSL2, where shell commands are sandboxed.
  A brewer or a check step runs as you here, so it can still read gh's login from the OS keyring or its hosts file. In WSL2, the sandboxed level hides your home folder and the other user folders from a brewer's shell commands, apart from the worktree, git's global configuration, parts of ~/.claude and the toolchain folders of serve's PATH, hides gh's hosts file where an absolute GH_CONFIG_DIR or XDG_CONFIG_HOME puts it, and the token variables, and keeps them from reaching GitHub; Claude Code's docs don't say whether the sandbox stops a read from the OS keyring. Under WSL, the user folders include the Windows user's logins under /mnt. For brewers, the container level mounts only the worktree, which closes both. A login a brewer did read is stopped at the push and left out of the pull request and the status comment when it looks like one. On native Windows, check steps run outside any sandbox, so they can still read gh's login.
Container: no Podman or Docker (optional)

Foedrum has everything it needs.

`;

exports[`foedrum doctor > reports what is missing, and exits 1 when a required tool is 1`] = `
Foedrum doctor

  node         24.20.0
  npm          11.6.2   /usr/bin/npm
  git          2.47.1   /usr/bin/git
  gh           2.102.0  /usr/bin/gh
  claude       missing
  bwrap        missing
  socat        1.7.4.4  /usr/bin/socat
  slirp4netns  1.2.1    /usr/bin/slirp4netns

Isolation: permissions only (the sandbox needs bubblewrap and socat)
  A brewer runs as you here, so it can still read gh's login from the OS keyring or its hosts file. The sandboxed level (bubblewrap and socat) hides your home folder and the other user folders from a brewer's shell commands, apart from the worktree, git's global configuration, parts of ~/.claude and the toolchain folders of serve's PATH, hides gh's hosts file where an absolute GH_CONFIG_DIR or XDG_CONFIG_HOME puts it, and the token variables, and keeps them from reaching GitHub; Claude Code's docs don't say whether the sandbox stops a read from the OS keyring. For brewers, the container level mounts only the worktree, which closes both. A login a brewer did read is stopped at the push and left out of the pull request and the status comment when it looks like one. Check steps run only in bubblewrap's sandbox, with no network and the home folder hidden; where it cannot start, they do not run.
Container: no Podman or Docker (optional)

Problems:
  claude not found (required): install Claude Code, https://code.claude.com/docs/en/setup
  bubblewrap (bwrap) not found: install bubblewrap for the sandboxed level

Foedrum cannot run brewers until the required problems are fixed.

`;

